---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Replaying and Evaluating AI Agent Improvement Loops · San Lee"
  description="A recorded-replay viewer for two autonomous improvement runs. It shows score progression, iteration edits, and the gap between training and held-out results."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/loop-replay.html"
  ogType="website"
  ogImage="/assets/og-loop-replay.png"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* ----- Page-specific styles only ----- */
    /* Everything reusable (badge, .decision, .article-body table.metrics, the
       .diagram frame) already lives in assets/style.css and is reused as-is
       below. What's here is unique to this viewer: the demo-data banner, the
       stat tiles, the chart's series colors, and the iteration nav. */

    /* Demo-data banner, set as a ruled folio line rather than a coloured pill.
       The old construction was a rounded amber chip with a status dot, and the
       amber was a FOURTH hue invented on this page -- against a palette that is
       deliberately two, whose second hue carries exactly one meaning: "the
       record of something that was built and then taken back" (style.css, the
       reversal block). A "do not read these numbers as a result" mark is that
       meaning in its sharpest form -- it withdraws the claim the figures below
       would otherwise be making. So the badge takes the oxide it should always
       have taken, and the invented hue goes with it.

       The shape follows the rest of the journal: a 2px rule with a caps label
       under it, the same grammar a .decision entry opens with. Where there is
       room the label and the sentence share the line and read as a folio line;
       at 320px the sentence wraps beneath it, which is the same idiom with less
       margin to spend. No fill, no radius, no status dot. */
    .demo-badge {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      align-items: baseline;
      gap: 4px 14px;
      border-top: 2px solid var(--accent-warm);
      padding: 9px 0 0;
      margin: 0 0 28px;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
    }
    .demo-badge .demo-badge-label {
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      color: var(--accent-warm);
    }
    /* 16rem basis: the sentence shares the label's line when the measure can
       hold it and drops to its own line when it cannot, with no breakpoint. */
    .demo-badge .demo-badge-text { flex: 1 1 16rem; }
    /* Real-run variant: the same folio line, drained of the oxide. The oxide
       above means "this is not a measured result"; once a measured run is
       loaded that withdrawal would itself be the lie, so the rule goes back to
       plain ink and the label simply states what is loaded. Applied by
       loop-replay.js when isDryRun is false. */
    .demo-badge.real-run { border-top-color: var(--rule-strong); }
    .demo-badge.real-run .demo-badge-label { color: var(--muted); }

    /* Run tabs: which recorded run plays. Drawn as a running head -- the same
       small tracked caps over a hairline that .doors uses for the site nav --
       rather than as a segmented control of filled pills. The two are the same
       object: a row of words, one of which is the one you are looking at. The
       site already has a treatment for that, and a page that invents a second
       one is claiming a distinction it does not have.

       The selected tab is marked exactly the way .doors marks aria-current:
       accent plus a 2px underline, so state never rests on colour alone. Here
       aria-pressed is the attribute (these are buttons swapping content in
       place, not links to another page), but it drives the identical pair.

       Buttons do not inherit font-family, so it is declared rather than
       assumed. min-height keeps the 44px tap target the caps type no longer
       supplies on its own. */
    .run-tabs {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 0 26px;
      margin: 0 0 14px;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
    .run-tabs button {
      min-height: 44px;
      padding: 0;
      border: 0;
      border-radius: 0;
      background: none;
      font-family: var(--font);
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.13em;
      cursor: pointer;
      transition: color 0.15s ease;
    }
    .run-tabs button:hover { color: var(--accent); }
    .run-tabs button[aria-pressed="true"] {
      color: var(--accent);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 6px;
      text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
    }
    .run-intro { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0 0 18px; }

    /* Run-summary figures: the site's .stat-strip, not a local grid of cards.
       This page used to draw its own bordered, filled, 10px-radius tiles on the
       argument that "label + big number" was different content. It is not --
       it is the same object the homepage presents its three headline accuracies
       as, and .stat-strip already draws it: an ink rule over each figure, a
       hairline under its label, no fill and no box, collapsing to a ruled list
       on a phone. Adopting it deletes the local construction entirely; what is
       left here is only what this page genuinely adds.

       TWO THINGS IT ADDS. First, .stat-sub -- a qualifier under the label
       ("iteration 2", "the honest number") that the homepage's three figures
       have no need for. Second, the series colour on the figure itself.

       The colour is deliberate and stays: --series-a/b/c are keyed to the
       legend above the chart and to the split map at the top of the page, so
       the colour on "Best C" is what tells a reader this figure is the gold
       held-out number without re-reading the label. That is a legend, not
       decoration, which is exactly the test the palette's restraint rule
       applies -- and why the amber banner failed it while these pass. */
    .stat-strip .stat-sub {
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      color: var(--muted);
    }
    .stat-strip li.stat-a .stat-value { color: var(--series-a); }
    .stat-strip li.stat-b .stat-value { color: var(--series-b); }
    .stat-strip li.stat-c .stat-value { color: var(--series-c); }
    /* The iteration strip re-renders on every scrub and carries three figures
       that are being COMPARED with each other, not read one at a time. At the
       global display size that row dominates the replay panel it is supposed to
       annotate, so it steps down a size. The construction -- rule, figure,
       label -- is unchanged, which is the part that had to match. */
    #iter-stats .stat-value { font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 1.6rem); }
    #iter-stats { margin-top: 14px; }

    /* Chart series colors. Kept separate from --accent since three series
       need to stay distinguishable at once; tuned per theme for contrast.

       "Tuned for contrast" was true of these as CHART STROKES, where the bar
       is 3:1 against the paper, and false of them as TEXT, which is what they
       also are: the honest-callout's figure, the diff's added lines, the stat
       values. The light --series-c was #16875a, which is 3.9:1 on paper and
       4.0:1 inside the callout -- under the 4.5:1 body-text floor on the two
       backgrounds it is actually read against. #13724c is the same hue taken
       down to 4.55:1 in the worst of those (the callout) and 5.3:1 on paper.
       It was darkened exactly as far as AA needs and no further, because the
       three series still have to be told apart from each other.

       --diff-del joins them because it was the same mistake in a sharper form:
       it was written as a bare #e5657a in the rule below, with no light value
       at all, so a colour picked to sit on a near-black panel was rendering on
       warm paper at 2.63:1. It is a per-theme token now, which is what stops
       the next colour from being added the same way. The light value is set
       level with --series-c's (4.9:1 vs 4.8:1 on the code block) so the two
       sides of a diff read as peers -- an added line and a removed line should
       not differ in weight, only in hue and in the +/- that carries the
       meaning when hue cannot. */
    :root, html[data-theme="dark"] {
      --series-a: #6ea8fe; /* A: training split -- what the loop optimizes on */
      --series-b: #f2b84b; /* B: held-back split -- what drives the stop signal */
      --series-c: #4ac48a; /* C: gold held-out -- the honest number */
      --diff-del: #e5657a;
    }
    html[data-theme="light"] {
      --series-a: #2563eb;
      --series-b: #b9790a;
      --series-c: #13724c;
      --diff-del: #be203a;
    }

    /* The A/B/C split map (portfolio ADR-004's third visual candidate). Lives
       here rather than in assets/style.css because it borrows --series-a/b/c,
       which are page-scoped above -- and that borrowing is the point: the
       reader learns the colour code from this diagram and carries it into the
       score chart further down. */
    .split-map {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 4px 0 10px;
    }
    /* Set boxes take the series colour; what they feed stays neutral, so the
       eye tracks the three sets rather than the six shapes. */
    .split-map .sm-set { fill: var(--bg); stroke-width: 2; }
    .split-map .sm-a { stroke: var(--series-a); }
    .split-map .sm-b { stroke: var(--series-b); }
    .split-map .sm-c { stroke: var(--series-c); }
    .split-map .sm-box { fill: var(--panel); stroke: var(--border); stroke-width: 1.5; }
    .split-map .sm-id { fill: var(--text); font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; }
    .split-map .sm-role { fill: var(--text); font-size: 12.5px; }
    /* max-width caps the SVG at 260px, which is under the content column even
       at a 320px viewport (272px inside the gutters) -- so it renders 1:1 at
       every width the gate tests and these viewBox units ARE CSS pixels.
       Measured, not assumed: scale came back 1.000 at 320, 390 and 900. */
    .split-map .sm-note { fill: var(--muted); font-size: 12px; }
    .split-map .sm-edge { fill: none; stroke: var(--muted); stroke-width: 1.5; }
    .split-map .sm-edge-a { stroke: var(--series-a); }
    .split-map marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .split-map marker path.sm-head-a { fill: var(--series-a); }
    .split-caption { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0 0 28px; }

    .chart-legend {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 16px;
      margin: 4px 0 14px;
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: var(--muted);
    }
    .chart-legend span { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; }
    .chart-legend .swatch { width: 12px; height: 12px; border-radius: var(--radius-lg); display: inline-block; }
    .chart-legend .swatch.a { background: var(--series-a); }
    .chart-legend .swatch.b { background: var(--series-b); }
    .chart-legend .swatch.c { background: var(--series-c); }

    #score-chart .gap-fill { fill: var(--series-c); opacity: 0.08; }
    #score-chart .series-line { fill: none; stroke-width: 2.5; }
    #score-chart .series-a { stroke: var(--series-a); }
    #score-chart .series-b { stroke: var(--series-b); }
    #score-chart .series-c { stroke: var(--series-c); }
    #score-chart .series-dot { cursor: pointer; }
    #score-chart .series-dot.a { fill: var(--series-a); }
    #score-chart .series-dot.b { fill: var(--series-b); }
    #score-chart .series-dot.c { fill: var(--series-c); }
    #score-chart .gridline { stroke: var(--border); stroke-width: 1; }
    #score-chart .axis-label { fill: var(--muted); font-size: 10px; }
    #score-chart .cursor-line { stroke: var(--text); stroke-width: 1; stroke-dasharray: 3 3; opacity: 0.5; }
    #score-chart .hit-col { fill: transparent; cursor: pointer; }
    #score-chart .hit-col:hover { fill: var(--accent-soft); }

    /* Iteration nav: prev/next either side of a centered slider + label,
       same 44px tap-target rule as the rest of the site. */
    .iter-nav {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 12px;
      margin: 18px 0 6px;
    }
    /* Squared and unfilled, to match every other control on the site. A 50%
       radius over a --panel fill is the circular icon button of an app
       toolbar; what this actually is, is a pair of marks either side of a
       position indicator. The 1px hairline is all the box it needs. */
    .iter-nav button {
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      width: 44px; height: 44px;
      border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      background: none;
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: 1.1rem;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .iter-nav button:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
    .iter-nav button:disabled { opacity: 0.35; cursor: default; }
    /* min-width: 0 overrides the flex-item default of min-width: auto, which
       otherwise refuses to shrink a <input type="range"> below its intrinsic
       content size -- the thing that was pushing this row past 320px. */
    .iter-nav input[type="range"] { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; accent-color: var(--accent); }
    .iter-nav .iter-label {
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      min-width: 92px;
      text-align: right;
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      color: var(--muted);
    }

    .iter-detail h3 { margin: 22px 0 8px; font-size: 1rem; }
    .iter-baseline-note {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--border);
      padding: 6px 12px;
      margin: 8px 0;
    }

    /* Diff view: reuse the site's monospace <pre> styling, add per-line color
       so +/- reads like a diff instead of plain text. */
    #iter-diff .diff-add { color: var(--series-c); }
    #iter-diff .diff-del { color: var(--diff-del); }
    #iter-diff .diff-hunk { color: var(--muted); }
    #iter-diff .diff-meta { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 600; }

    /* The honesty callout, un-carded like everything else. The rounded right
       corners and the --accent-soft wash made this a tinted card with a stripe;
       what carries the meaning is the rule, and the rule is in --series-c
       because this sentence is about the gold held-out number. Dropping the
       wash also takes the figure inside it off a second background -- the
       page's --series-c value had to be darkened to clear 4.5:1 against this
       fill specifically, and it is now read on plain paper.

       Matches .iter-baseline-note directly below it, which was already the
       system's answer to "an aside with a rule". */
    .honest-callout {
      border-left: 3px solid var(--series-c);
      border-radius: 0;
      padding: 6px 12px;
      margin: 18px 0;
    }
    .honest-callout strong { color: var(--series-c); }

    /* ----- The figure contract's two caption slots (ADR-013) -----
       ORIGIN COPY. No page shipped a data-fig caption before this one, so
       there was nothing to copy: a later lane copies this block verbatim and
       names loop-replay.astro and its commit in the comment above its copy.

       Page-scoped rather than assets/style.css because a concurrent session
       owns that file for the tap-target work, and two writers on one
       generated surface cannot be merged. OC-F consolidates the copies after
       that session merges.

       The tokens are .split-caption's, deliberately: this page already draws
       a caption in --muted at 0.92rem directly above, and a second caption
       grammar on one page would read as a second kind of claim. --muted is
       also a real colour token rather than a faded one -- opacity never dims
       text on this site (ADR-009), and contrast-check.cjs reads a figcaption
       because it is HTML and not SVG text. */
    .fig { margin: 0; }
    .fig figcaption { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 4px 0 28px; }
    /* Both slots are blocks so the limit reads as its own line rather than as
       a tail the eye can run past. The gate requires both to be non-empty;
       this is what makes the limit legible as well as required. */
    .fig .fig-what, .fig .fig-limit { display: block; }
    .fig .fig-limit { margin-top: 6px; }

    /* lr1-pipeline. The prefix is lr1- on every class and on the marker id,
       so nothing collides with the split map's sm-/split-arrow set above. A
       duplicate marker id repaints the wrong arrow head and no gate sees it.

       NEUTRAL ONLY, and that is a decision rather than a default. --accent-warm
       carries exactly one meaning on this site, "a decision that was taken
       back", and nothing in this plate is a reversal. --series-a/b/c are keyed
       to the A/B/C split map above and to the chart below, and this plate is
       about log transport, not about splits -- borrowing one would claim a
       relationship the figure does not have. So the plate reads in the same
       ink as the body, and every state it draws is a word, not a hue.

       max-width caps the SVG at 260px, which is under the content column even
       at a 320px viewport, so these viewBox units are CSS pixels 1:1 -- the
       same basis the split map above records. SVG text inherits font-family
       from <body> rather than from the page, so each text class declares it. */
    .lr1-plate {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 4px 0 10px;
    }
    .lr1-plate .lr1-box { fill: var(--panel); stroke: var(--border); stroke-width: 1.5; }
    .lr1-plate .lr1-name { fill: var(--text); font-family: var(--font); font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; }
    .lr1-plate .lr1-key { fill: var(--muted); font-family: var(--font); font-size: 11.5px; }
    .lr1-plate .lr1-step { fill: var(--text); font-family: var(--font); font-size: 12px; }
    .lr1-plate .lr1-note { fill: var(--muted); font-family: var(--font); font-size: 11px; }
    .lr1-plate .lr1-edge { fill: none; stroke: var(--muted); stroke-width: 1.5; }
    /* A marker does not inherit the referencing path's stroke, so the head is
       painted to match the edge rather than left to chance. */
    .lr1-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
  </style>
  </Fragment>


  <SiteNav current="work" />


  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="defense-news-classifier.html">&larr; Back to the classifier</a>
    <h1>Loop replay</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      This page is a recorded-replay viewer for the autonomous loop&rsquo;s run
      logs. It covers both rungs of the
      <a href="defense-news-classifier.html#autonomy-ladder">L3 step</a>
      of the classifier&rsquo;s autonomy ladder. The two rungs are the
      prompt-optimization loop and the agent-driven ML loop.
    </p>

    <p class="lead">
      The loop optimizes against one set. Two other sets score the loop, and the
      loop never touches them. This page replays a run, so the chart shows the
      gap between &ldquo;improved on A&rdquo; and &ldquo;improved on C&rdquo;.
    </p>

    <!-- The three-way split, drawn. All three sets are scored every iteration;
         what differs is what each one is allowed to influence, and only A's
         arrow comes back. That asymmetry IS the overfitting guard, and it was
         previously three sentences of topology the reader had to assemble. The
         set colours match the chart's series colours below, so the code is
         learned here and carried down the page. -->
    <svg class="split-map" viewBox="0 0 260 184" role="img"
         aria-label="Set A feeds the agent's prompt rewrite and is re-scored each iteration, a closed loop. Set B is scored and stops the run. Set C is scored and reported only. Neither B nor C feeds back into the prompt.">
      <defs>
        <marker id="split-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
        <!-- A's return edge gets its own marker so the head takes the series-a
             colour with the line; markers don't inherit the path's stroke. -->
        <marker id="split-arrow-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
          <path class="sm-head-a" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- rx matches --radius-sm (2px). The max-width note above is why these
           viewBox units are CSS pixels 1:1, so 2 here is 2px on the page. -->
      <rect class="sm-set sm-a" x="4" y="10" width="44" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="sm-id" x="26" y="34" text-anchor="middle">A</text>
      <path class="sm-edge sm-edge-a" d="M 48 28 L 70 28" marker-end="url(#split-arrow-a)" />
      <rect class="sm-box" x="74" y="10" width="182" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="sm-role" x="165" y="33" text-anchor="middle">drives the next edit</text>
      <!-- The one edge that comes back. Its absence on B and C is the argument. -->
      <path class="sm-edge sm-edge-a" d="M 165 46 C 165 76, 26 76, 26 50"
            marker-end="url(#split-arrow-a)" />
      <text class="sm-note" x="96" y="90" text-anchor="middle">re-scored every iteration</text>

      <rect class="sm-set sm-b" x="4" y="104" width="44" height="32" rx="2" />
      <text class="sm-id" x="26" y="126" text-anchor="middle">B</text>
      <path class="sm-edge" d="M 48 120 L 70 120" marker-end="url(#split-arrow)" />
      <rect class="sm-box" x="74" y="104" width="182" height="32" rx="2" />
      <text class="sm-role" x="165" y="125" text-anchor="middle">stops the run</text>

      <rect class="sm-set sm-c" x="4" y="146" width="44" height="32" rx="2" />
      <text class="sm-id" x="26" y="168" text-anchor="middle">C</text>
      <path class="sm-edge" d="M 48 162 L 70 162" marker-end="url(#split-arrow)" />
      <rect class="sm-box" x="74" y="146" width="182" height="32" rx="2" />
      <text class="sm-role" x="165" y="167" text-anchor="middle">reported only</text>
    </svg>

    <p class="split-caption">
      Only <strong>A</strong>&rsquo;s arrow comes back. <strong>B</strong> is
      held out of that feedback loop so stop-detection stays honest, and
      <strong>C</strong> &mdash; real gold text &mdash; never enters the
      agent&rsquo;s context at all.
    </p>

    <div class="run-tabs" id="run-tabs" aria-label="Choose a recorded run">
      <button type="button" aria-pressed="true">Rung 1 &middot; prompt loop</button>
      <button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Rung 2 &middot; ML loop</button>
    </div>
    <p class="run-intro" id="run-intro"><!-- filled by JS --></p>

    <div class="demo-badge" id="demo-badge">
      <span class="demo-badge-label" id="demo-badge-label">Real run</span>
      <span class="demo-badge-text" id="demo-badge-text">claude-sonnet-5, 1,524,937 tokens, stopped on the <code>threshold</code> done-signal. Numbers below are a measured result, not a demo.</span>
    </div>

    <h2>Run summary</h2>
    <ul class="stat-strip wide" id="summary-stats">
      <!-- filled by JS -->
    </ul>
    <div class="honest-callout" id="honest-callout">
      <!-- filled by JS -->
    </div>

    <h2>Score progression</h2>
    <p>
      <span id="chart-metric-line">Category macro-F1 per split, per iteration.</span>
      The shaded band is the gap between A and C. A wider band means the loop
      improved on the set it optimizes against. It also means the loop did not
      improve on real gold text.
    </p>
    <div class="chart-legend wide">
      <span><i class="swatch a"></i>A &middot; training (optimized on)</span>
      <span><i class="swatch b"></i>B &middot; held-back (stops the loop)</span>
      <span><i class="swatch c"></i>C &middot; gold held-out (the honest number)</span>
    </div>
    <svg id="score-chart" class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 640 280" role="img" aria-label="Line chart of category macro-F1 for splits A, B, and C across iterations"></svg>

    <h2>Iteration replay</h2>
    <div class="iter-nav">
      <button id="iter-prev" type="button" aria-label="Previous iteration">&lsaquo;</button>
      <input type="range" id="iter-slider" min="0" max="0" value="0" step="1" aria-label="Iteration" />
      <button id="iter-next" type="button" aria-label="Next iteration">&rsaquo;</button>
      <span class="iter-label" id="iter-label">Iteration 0 / 0</span>
    </div>

    <div class="iter-detail">
      <ul class="stat-strip" id="iter-stats"><!-- filled by JS --></ul>

      <div id="iter-baseline" class="iter-baseline-note" hidden>
        Iteration 0 is the baseline. The loop scores it before any edit, so it
        has no diff, no rationale, and no edit summary yet.
      </div>

      <div id="iter-edit-block">
        <h3>Agent rationale</h3>
        <p id="iter-rationale"></p>

        <h3>Edit summary</h3>
        <p id="iter-summary"></p>

        <h3 id="iter-diff-heading">Prompt diff</h3>
        <pre id="iter-diff"></pre>
      </div>

      <p class="ex-note" id="iter-meta"></p>
    </div>

    <h2>The decisions</h2>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Decision</p>
      <h3>Replay iteration-by-iteration, not just a summary chart</h3>
      <p>A single before/after number hides <strong>when</strong> A and C
        diverge, and which prompt edit caused it. This page steps through every
        iteration&rsquo;s scores, diff, and rationale. The chart and the detail
        panel move together.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> the loop spec is about a
        trajectory, not an endpoint. A viewer that shows only the final delta
        cannot show a run that overfit early and then recovered. It cannot show
        a run that looked correct on B until C found it.
        <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> this viewer needs more UI than a static
        chart. I kept it to plain HTML/CSS/JS with no chart library, to match
        the rest of the site&rsquo;s stack.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Decision</p>
      <h3>Build against a labeled mock, then swap in the real run</h3>
      <p>This viewer shipped first against a <code>--dry-run</code> mock,
        because the viewer was the deliverable and a real run costs real money.
        The mock is a deterministic offline backend with zero API calls, and it
        prefixes every rationale field <code>[dry-run]</code>. A banner labeled
        the mock as a mock, and named it. The real run finished the next day,
        and the page now loads it: 1.5M tokens, <code>threshold</code> stop at
        iteration 2.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> nobody can review a viewer that
        needs real data before anyone can build it. A mock left in place after
        real data arrives is a stale claim. The single swappable data file made
        both statements true.
        <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> the swap needed a CSS fix that the original
        plan missed. The &ldquo;real run&rdquo; state had a class but no style.
        The banner would therefore have kept its warning colour and claimed a
        measured result at the same time. The fix was cheap once I exercised
        the swap.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>How this reads a run log</h2>

    <!-- lr1-pipeline. The two rungs' dialects are the only part of this
         section that is topology: two logs in, one normalizer, one viewer.
         Everything else here is a log shape, a transport decision, and a
         procedure, so it stays as the prose and the list below.

         The plate carries the tag key that tells the two dialects apart
         (type against record) and the negative half of the claim (neither log
         is edited). The rung numbers and the two diff shapes are in .fig-what,
         because a hand-drawn figure carries no digit (ADR-013) and because a
         caption is read by contrast-check.cjs while SVG text is not. -->
    <figure class="fig" data-fig="plate">
      <svg class="lr1-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 132" role="img"
           aria-label="Two run logs enter in different dialects. One tags its records with type. The other tags with record. Both reach one normalizer at parse time, and then the viewer. Neither log is edited.">
        <defs>
          <marker id="lr1-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                  markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto">
            <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <rect class="lr1-box" x="2" y="2" width="124" height="42" rx="2" />
        <text class="lr1-name" x="64" y="21" text-anchor="middle">prompt loop</text>
        <text class="lr1-key" x="64" y="36" text-anchor="middle">tags: type</text>

        <rect class="lr1-box" x="134" y="2" width="124" height="42" rx="2" />
        <text class="lr1-name" x="196" y="21" text-anchor="middle">ML loop</text>
        <text class="lr1-key" x="196" y="36" text-anchor="middle">tags: record</text>

        <!-- One path draws both drops and the join between them, so the two
             logs read as one convergence and not as two unrelated edges. -->
        <path class="lr1-edge" d="M 64 44 L 64 53 L 196 53 L 196 44" />
        <path class="lr1-edge" d="M 130 53 L 130 60" marker-end="url(#lr1-arrow)" />

        <rect class="lr1-box" x="26" y="62" width="208" height="40" rx="2" />
        <text class="lr1-step" x="130" y="81" text-anchor="middle">normalize at parse time</text>
        <text class="lr1-note" x="130" y="96" text-anchor="middle">neither log is edited</text>

        <path class="lr1-edge" d="M 130 102 L 130 109" marker-end="url(#lr1-arrow)" />

        <rect class="lr1-box" x="82" y="111" width="96" height="20" rx="2" />
        <text class="lr1-step" x="130" y="125" text-anchor="middle">the viewer</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">Two run logs in different dialects reach one
          viewer. Rung 1 tags records with <code>type</code> and writes
          unified prompt diffs. Rung 2 tags with <code>record</code> and writes
          one-line experiment diffs.</span>
        <span class="fig-limit">The plate does not show the log shape or the
          transport. The paragraph below keeps both, and it keeps the CORS
          reason.</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The run log is <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/evals/optimize/README.md">append-only
      JSONL</a>: a <code>run_metadata</code> line, one <code>iteration</code>
      line per step, and a trailing <code>run_summary</code>. This page ships
      the run log as a plain JS string
      (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/projects/data/loop-replay-real.js"><code>projects/data/loop-replay-real.js</code></a>).
      It does not fetch a <code>.jsonl</code> file. The page renders the same
      way from disk and from GitHub Pages. A <code>fetch()</code> of a local
      file hits browser CORS restrictions that a plain
      <code>&lt;script src&gt;</code> does not.
    </p>

    <p>
      Rung 2&rsquo;s run ships the same way in a second file
      (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/portfolio/blob/main/projects/data/loop-replay-run2-real.js"><code>loop-replay-run2-real.js</code></a>).
      The classifier repo gitignores its source log, so the embed here is the
      published record. Each run carries its own dry-run flag, so the page
      cannot show a mock without its warning banner.
    </p>

    <p>To add another run:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the file in <code>projects/data/</code>.</li>
      <li>Regenerate a template literal from its raw text.</li>
      <li>Register it in <code>loop-replay.js</code>&rsquo;s run list.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      This viewer does not cover L4&rsquo;s multi-agent audit trail. That log is
      event-shaped: triage evidence, a challenge, a backward bounce, and a
      verdict, per row. It is not an iteration-by-iteration score curve. A
      replay through this player&rsquo;s chart would flatten the structure the
      log exists to keep. Its measured result lives in the classifier repo
      (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/evals/l4_eval.txt"><code>evals/l4_eval.txt</code></a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/020-l4-multi-agent-pipeline.md">ADR-020</a>).
      If it gets a viewer, that viewer is a separate page.
    </p>

    <h2>Stack</h2>
    <p>
      The page uses vanilla HTML/CSS/JS, and it matches the rest of this site.
      It uses no chart library and no build step. The chart is a hand-built SVG,
      with the same <code>createElementNS</code> pattern as the
      <a href="the-system.html">system diagram</a>. The page parses the run log
      as plain JSONL, with <code>JSON.parse</code> per line.
    </p>
  </main>

